0366 | \begin{proof}[Proof of Proposition \ref{prop:strands}]
0367 | 	We start by considering $\Gamma^+$. Let $\pi$ be canonical the
0368 | 	surjection of $\Gamma$ onto $\bbZ$ or $\bbZ\rtimes \bbZ/2\bbZ$, and
0369 | 	let $K = \ker(\pi)$. Let $g \in \Gamma^+$ be an element that maps to
0370 | 	a generator of the maximal infinite cyclic subgroup. $K$ is finite
0371 | 	and we have $\Gamma^+=\bk{g,K}$. Conjugation by $g$ induces an
0372 | 	automorphism of the finite group $K$ so if $n$ is the exponent of
0373 | 	$\Aut K$ then $g^n$ commutes with every element in $K$. Since $g^n$
0374 | 	commutes with $g$ as well we conclude that $g^n$ is central in
0375 | 	$\Gamma^+$. It follows from Theorem \ref{thm:bh-isom}
0376 | 	(\ref{it:things-that-commute}) that $\Gamma^+ = \bk{g,K}$ leaves
0377 | 	$\mathrm{Min}(g^n) = Y\times \bbR$ invariant.
0378 | 
0379 | 	Again, by Theorem \ref{thm:bh-isom} (\ref{it:things-that-commute}),
0380 | 	every $\gamma \in \Gamma^+$ projects to an isometry $\alpha'_\gamma$
0381 | 	of $Y$, which itself is a $\cato$ metric space. Since $X$ is 2-ended
0382 | 	and $Y\times \bbR$ is a convex subset of $X$, $Y$ must be
0383 | 	bounded. Let $\Gamma'=\bk{\alpha'_\gamma \mid \gamma\in \Gamma^+}$
0384 | 	and $Y_0\subset Y$ be the set of points fixed pointwise by all
0385 | 	elements of $\Gamma'$. Proposition \ref{prop:cato-centre} implies
0386 | 	that $Y$ has a centre $c_Y$ which must be fixed by $\Gamma'$ so
0387 | 	$Y_0$ is nonempty.
0388 | 
0389 | 	\emph{Claim: The subset $Y_0 \times \bbR \subset X$ is independent
0390 | 		of the choice of $g$.} Let $g'$ be another lift of the image of
0391 | 	$g$. Then $(g')^n = g^nk$ for some $k \in K$. $(g')^n$ maps
0392 | 	$\mathrm{Min}(g^n)$ to itself, but it may permute axes. Let $(g')^n$
0393 | 	act on $Y\times \bbR$ as $(\beta',\beta'')$ where $\beta''$ must be
0394 | 	translation by $|g^n|$. By definition of $Y_0$,
0395 | 	$Y_0\times \bbR \subset \mathrm{Min}\left((g')^n\right)$ since
0396 | 	$\beta'$ acts trivially on $Y_0$. On the other hand if we repeat the
0397 | 	construction with $(g')^n$ to get
0398 | 	$Y'_0\times \bbR \subset \mathrm{Min}\left((g')^n\right)$ we see
0399 | 	that every element of $Y'_0 \times \bbR$ must lie
0400 | 	$\mathrm{Min}(g^n)$ and in fact that
0401 | 	$Y'_0 \times \bbR \subset Y_0\times \bbR$, so by symmetry both sets
0402 | 	are equal. This completes the claim.
0403 | 
0404 | 	If $\Gamma = \Gamma^+$, i.e. $\Gamma$ is of $\bbZ$-type, then we
0405 | 	take $\sigma = \{c_{Y_0}\}\times\bbR \subset Y_0\times \bbR$, where
0406 | 	$c_{Y_0}$ is the centre of $Y_0$ in the sense of Proposition
0407 | 	\ref{prop:cato-centre}, and we are done. We now turn to the dihedral
0408 | 	case.  Let $r \in \Gamma$ be such that $\Gamma = \bk{\Gamma^+,r}$,
0409 | 	that is to say that $\pi(r) = \bar r$ is a reflection in
0410 | 	$D_\infty$. Let $x \in Y_0\times \bbR$ and let
0411 | 	$\rho_x = \{p\} \times \bbR$ be the $g^n$-axis containing $x$.
0412 | 
0413 | 	\emph{Claim: $r\cdot \rho_x \subset \mathrm{Min}(g^n)$.} We need to
0414 | 	show that $g^n$ translates $r\cdot x$ minimally. Note that
0415 | 	conjugation by $r$ gives an inverse modulo $k$ of $g^n$. Consider
0416 | 	the following:
0417 | 	\begin{align*}
0418 | 	g^n r \cdot x & = r (r^{-1}g^n r) \cdot x\\
0419 | 	& = r g^{-n} k \cdot x\\
0420 | 	& = r g^{-n} \cdot x
0421 | 	\end{align*}
0422 | 	This means that $g^n$ sends the point $r\cdot x \in r \cdot \rho_x$
0423 | 	to $r g^{-n} \cdot x \in r \cdot \rho_x$. In particular
0424 | 	$d(r\cdot x,g^{n}r\cdot x) = d(x,g^{-n}\cdot x)$, which means that
0425 | 	$r\cdot x$ realizes the translation length of $g^n$. This proves the
0426 | 	claim.
0427 | 
0428 | 	Now let $r'$ be another element such that $\pi(r) = \pi(r')$. Then
0429 | 	$r' = k'r = rk$ for some $k,k' \in K$. On one hand
0430 | 	$rk'\cdot \rho_x = r\cdot \rho_x = k r \cdot \rho_x$. Note that
0431 | 	$k\in K$ can be chosen arbitrarily. Note also that
0432 | 	\[gr \cdot \rho_x = r g^{-1} k \cdot \rho_x = r \cdot \rho_x, \] for
0433 | 	some $k \in K$. It follows that $r \cdot \rho_x$, like $\rho_x$, is
0434 | 	$\Gamma^+$-invariant and therefore in $Y_0\times \bbR$; thus
0435 | 	$Y_0\times \bbR$ is $\Gamma = \bk{r,\Gamma^+}$-invariant. Consider
0436 | 	the projection $Y_0 \times\bbR \onto Y_0$. Since $\Gamma^+$ fixes
0437 | 	fibres and $r$ maps fibres to fibres there is a canonical projection
0438 | 	$\Gamma \to \Isom{Y_0}$. Again there is a subset $Y_1 \subset Y_0$
0439 | 	that is fixed pointwise by the image of $\Gamma$. By Proposition
0440 | 	\ref{prop:cato-centre} there is a unique centre $c_{Y_1} \in Y_1$
0441 | 	and the strand $\sigma = \{c_{Y_1}\}\times \bbR \subset X$ is
0442 | 	canonical and $\Gamma$-invariant.
0443 | \end{proof}
