0571 | \begin{prop}\label{prop:quadrilaterals}
0572 |   Consider a quadrilateral $Q$ contained in the horostrip
0573 |   $\panel(y_1,y_2)$ whose boundary is given by the
0574 |   union\[ \alpha \cup \ell_1 \cup \beta \cup \ell_2
0575 |   \] where $\alpha, \beta \subset \partial \panel(y_1,y_2)$ and
0576 |   $\ell_1,\ell_2$ are vertical leaves. Then, with respect to the
0577 |   metric induced by $\bbH^2$, $\ell_1,\ell_2$ are geodesics with
0578 |   length\[
0579 |     \left|\ln\left(\frac{\mathrm{length}(\alpha)}{\mathrm{length}(\beta)}\right)\right|
0580 |     = \mathrm{width}(\panel(y_1,y_2)).\]
0581 | \end{prop}
0582 | \begin{proof}
0583 |   If $\gamma(t) = (x(t),y(t))$ is a parametric curve
0584 |   $\gamma: [a,b] \to \{(x,y) \in \bbR^2 \mid y>0\}$ and
0585 |   $\gamma = \gamma([a,b])$ is a path in $\bbH^2$, then its length is
0586 |   given by
0587 |   \[ \mathrm{length}(\gamma) = \int_\gamma ds_{\bbH^2}=
0588 |     \int_a^b\frac{\sqrt{x'(t)^2+y'(t)^2}}{y(t)}dt.\] Without loss of
0589 |   generality $\alpha \subset \{(x,y) \mid y=y_1\}$ and $y_1<y_2$. The result now
0590 |   follows from the paramaterizations:
0591 |   \begin{eqnarray*}
0592 |     \alpha(t) &=& (t,y_1); t \in [a,b]\\
0593 |     \ell_1(t) &=& (b,t); t\in [y_1,y_2]\\
0594 |     \beta(t) &=& (t,y_2); t \in [a,b]\\
0595 |     \ell_2(t) &=& (a,t); t \in [y_1,y_2]\\
0596 |   \end{eqnarray*}
0597 |   and some calculus.
0598 | \end{proof}
