1297 | \begin{defn}\label{defn:flapping}
1298 |   Denote by $G^0_\churro \leq G_\churro$ the subgroup of elements that
1299 |   act trivially on the churro core $\churrocore$. An element
1300 |   $g \in G^0_\churro$ is called \define{purely flapping.}
1301 | \end{defn}
1302 | 
1303 | \begin{defn}[Flap families]\label{defn:flap-family}
1304 |   We say that two flaps $\flap_1,\flap_2 \subset \churro$ in a churro
1305 |   are in the same \define{flap family} if there is some purely flapping
1306 |   $g \in G^0_\churro$ such that $g\cdot \flap_1=\flap_2$. We denote by
1307 |   $\flapfam\flap$ the set of flaps in the flap family of $\flap$.
1308 | \end{defn}
1309 | 
1310 | \begin{defn}[Churro class]\label{defn:churro-class}
1311 |   We say two flaps $\flap_1,\flap_2 \subset \churro$ are in the same
1312 |   \define{churro class}, $\churroclass{\flap_1}$, if there is some $g$
1313 |   in $G_\churro$ such that $g\cdot \flap_1 = \flap_2$.  We say that
1314 |   two flap families $\flapfam 1,\flapfam 2$ are in the same
1315 |   \define{churro class} $\churroclass{\flapfam 1}$ if there is some
1316 |   $g \in G_\churro$ such that $g\cdot \flapfam 1 = \flapfam 2$.
1317 | \end{defn}
1318 | 
1319 | In this way $G_\churro$ acts by permutations on the collection of flap families. The
1320 | following is obvious.
1321 | 
1322 | \begin{lem}\label{lem:core-action}
1323 |   If $g \in G_\churro$ acts
1324 | 
1325 |   non-trivially on the collection of flap families then it
1326 |   acts non-trivially on $\churrocore.$
1327 | \end{lem}
1328 | 
1329 | We will now construct a finitely generated group $\basicgp{\churro}$
1330 | equipped with an action $\basicgp\churro \actson \churro$ that gives a
1331 | discrete grouping of $\churro \onto G_\churro \backslash
1332 | \churro$. Informally, it will be a direct product of a group, either
1333 | $\bbZ$ or $\bbZ\semidirect\bbZ_2$, acting on the churro core
1334 | $\churrocore$ and some finite groups acting transitively on flap
1335 | families. For reasons that will only become fully apparent in Section
1336 | \ref{sec:graph-with-cycles} it will be advantageous to use a specific
1337 | class of finite groups.
1338 | 
1339 | 
1340 | 
1341 | \begin{defn}[Low power abelian groups]\label{defn:lowpower}
1342 |   Let $n$ be a positive integer and let $n=p_1^{e_1}\cdots p_k^{e_k}$
1343 |   be its prime decomposition. The \define{low power abelian group of
1344 |     order $n$} is the group\[
1345 |     \lowpower n = \bigoplus_{i=1}^k\left(\bigoplus_{j=1}^{e_k} C_{p_i}\right),
1346 |     \] where $C_{p_i}$ is the cyclic group of order $p_i$.
1347 | \end{defn}
1348 | 
1349 | \begin{lem}\label{lem:lowpower}
1350 |   Low power abelian groups satisfy the following properties:
1351 |   \begin{itemize}
1352 |   \item $\lowpower n$ acts freely and transitively on any set of order
1353 |     $n$.
1354 |   \item The class of low power abelian groups is closed under direct products and passing to subgroups.
1355 |   \item Within the class of low power abelian groups, isomorphism is
1356 |     determined by cardinality.
1357 |   \end{itemize}
1358 | \end{lem}
1359 | \begin{proof}
1360 |   The first assertion is a consequence of Cayley's Theorem, the second
1361 |   assertion follows from the definition, the third assertion is an
1362 |   immediate consequence of the classification of finitely generated
1363 |   abelian groups and the prime decomposition theorem.
1364 | \end{proof}
